# `operators::divergence`

The divergence of a vector field: `Σ ∂Fᵢ/∂xᵢ`, a scalar function.

## Signature

```cpp
operators::Operator operators::divergence(pluss::StackArray<std::string_view> variables, operators::DiffOptions options = {});
```

## What It Does

`divergence({"x", "y", "z"})` is the operator that takes a vector field `F = (F₁, F₂, F₃)` (a rank-1 function with one component per coordinate) and returns the scalar function `∂F₁/∂x + ∂F₂/∂y + ∂F₃/∂z`. The divergence is the net outflow per unit volume: positive where the field spreads out (a source), negative where it converges (a sink), zero for an incompressible flow. It is the left side of every conservation law, `∂ρ/∂t + ∇·(ρv) = 0`.

`variables` names the coordinates, in order; the components of a vector field are listed in the same order, so a field in three dimensions has three components and the coordinates `{"x", "y", "z"}`. The field must have exactly one component per coordinate, or the operator is refused.

## Parameters

`pluss::StackArray<std::string_view> variables`
: The coordinates, in the order of the field's components.

`operators::DiffOptions options`
: Non-smooth policy, fallback and size limit.

## Returns

An `Operator` whose application returns the divergence as a scalar `Function`.

## Errors

- `the field needs one component per coordinate`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

The divergence of `F = (xy, yz, zx)` is `y + z + x`, which is `6` at `(1, 2, 3)`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function F = math::function({"x", "y", "z"}, [](math::Function x, math::Function y, math::Function z) { return math::stack({x * y, y * z, z * x}); });
  const auto point = {pluss::make_series(1.0), pluss::make_series(2.0), pluss::make_series(3.0)};

  const math::Function div = operators::divergence({"x", "y", "z"})(F);
  out.writeln("rank", div.rank(), "  div F at (1, 2, 3) =", div(point).item<double>(), "  (x + y + z = 6)");
}
```

### Example 2

Sources, sinks and rotations. The radial field `(x, y, z)` has divergence 3 everywhere; the rotation `(−y, x, 0)` has divergence 0 (incompressible); the inverse-square field `(x, y, z)/r³` is divergence-free away from the origin.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(8));
  const auto div3 = operators::divergence({"x", "y", "z"});
  const math::Function radial = math::function({"x", "y", "z"}, [](math::Function x, math::Function y, math::Function z) { return math::stack({x, y, z}); });
  const math::Function rotation = math::function({"x", "y", "z"}, [](math::Function x, math::Function y, math::Function z) { return math::stack({-y, x, z * 0.0}); });
  const math::Function inverse_square = math::function({"x", "y", "z"}, [](math::Function x, math::Function y, math::Function z) {
    const math::Function r3 = (x * x + y * y + z * z).pow(1.5);
    return math::stack({x / r3, y / r3, z / r3});
  });
  const auto point = {pluss::make_series(1.0), pluss::make_series(2.0), pluss::make_series(3.0)};
  out.writeln("radial:          ", div3(radial)(point).item<double>());
  out.writeln("|rotation|:      ", std::abs(div3(rotation)(point).item<double>()));
  out.writeln("|inverse square|:", std::abs(div3(inverse_square)(point).item<double>()));
}
```

### Example 3

An identity: the divergence of a curl is zero for every field. Composing the two operators on `F = (xy, yz, zx)` gives a function that is zero everywhere (here at `(1, 2, 3)`).

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(10));
  const math::Function F = math::function({"x", "y", "z"}, [](math::Function x, math::Function y, math::Function z) { return math::stack({x * y, y * z, z * x}); });
  const auto point = {pluss::make_series(1.0), pluss::make_series(2.0), pluss::make_series(3.0)};

  const operators::Operator div_of_curl = operators::compose(operators::divergence({"x", "y", "z"}), operators::curl({"x", "y", "z"}));
  out.writeln("div curl F =", div_of_curl(F)(point).item<double>());
}
```

### Example 4

The checks: one component per coordinate, so a scalar function or a field with too few components is refused.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const auto first_line = [](const pluss::Error &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    return text.substr(0, text.find('\n'));
  };

  const math::Function scalar = math::function({"x", "y", "z"}, [](math::Function x, math::Function y, math::Function z) { return x * y * z; });
  try { (void)operators::divergence({"x", "y", "z"})(scalar); } catch (const pluss::Error &e) { out.writeln("scalar input:    ", first_line(e)); }
  const math::Function planar = math::function({"x", "y", "z"}, [](math::Function x, math::Function y, math::Function) { return math::stack({x, y}); });
  try { (void)operators::divergence({"x", "y", "z"})(planar); } catch (const pluss::Error &e) { out.writeln("two components:  ", first_line(e)); }
}
```

